  { q: "What is the difference between BMR and TDEE maintenance calories?", a: "BMR is the base energy required solely for organ function and cell survival while completely motionless in bed. TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) adds physical movement, exercise, and the thermic effect of food on top of your BMR to determine your actual daily maintenance calories." },
  { q: "Can you eat below your BMR to lose weight?", a: "Eating below your BMR is generally not recommended for extended periods. When caloric intake drops chronically below BMR, the body downregulates non-exercise movement (NEAT), decreases thyroid hormone production, and breaks down skeletal muscle tissue." },
  { q: "What is a typical BMR for adult men and women?", a: "A typical BMR for adult women ranges between 1,200 and 1,550 kcal/day, whereas adult men generally burn between 1,550 and 2,000 kcal/day depending on height, weight, age, and lean muscle mass." },
  { q: "Is BMR the same as RMR (Resting Metabolic Rate)?", a: "They are nearly identical for practical planning. True BMR requires a strict overnight fast in a laboratory clinical setting, whereas RMR is measured under relaxed conditions with light digestion. RMR is usually roughly 5% to 10% higher than BMR." },
];
