  <MedicalNote />

  <p>Your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) is the exact number of calories your body burns at complete physiological rest just to maintain biological life. Even if you spent an entire 24-hour day lying motionless in bed without moving a muscle, your internal organs would still consume this energy keeping your heart beating, lungs breathing, kidneys filtering, and brain firing. For most individuals, BMR accounts for 60% to 70% of total daily energy expenditure—making it the essential foundation for setting accurate weight loss and bulking calories.</p>

  <h2>What is the Difference Between BMR and TDEE Maintenance Calories?</h2>
  <p>One of the most frequent points of confusion in nutrition is distinguishing <strong>BMR vs. TDEE</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR):</strong> Represents your cellular baseline. It counts zero movement, zero exercise, and zero digestion. It is strictly your biological survival floor.</li>
    <li><strong>Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE):</strong> Represents your true maintenance calories. It takes your BMR and adds the energy burned from digestion (<a href="/guides/thermic-effect-of-food-tef/">TEF</a>), non-exercise physical movement (<a href="/guides/what-is-neat-calories/">NEAT</a>), and intentional workouts (<a href="/tdee-calculator/">exercise expenditure</a>).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To lose fat safely, your calorie deficit should be deducted from your <strong>TDEE</strong>, not directly from your BMR. Setting your daily calorie intake below your BMR can provoke adaptive thermogenesis and cause unwanted muscle loss.</p>

  <h2>BMR vs. RMR: What is the Practical Difference?</h2>
  <p>Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) and Resting Metabolic Rate (RMR) are related clinical metrics measured under slightly different laboratory standards:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>BMR</strong> is measured in a darkened, temperature-regulated clinical laboratory immediately upon waking after 8 hours of sleep and 12 hours of total fasting, with the individual lying completely still.</li>
    <li><strong>RMR</strong> does not mandate overnight hospital stays or 12-hour fasts; it measures resting expenditure under normal daily conditions. Because slight food digestion or prior movement is present, RMR is typically 5% to 10% higher than BMR.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In standard sports nutrition and online fitness tracking, the Mifflin-St Jeor formula estimates resting expenditure and is used as the universal foundation for daily calorie planning.</p>

  <h2>How to Calculate BMR Manually (Mifflin-St Jeor Formula for Men vs. Women)</h2>
  <p>The gold-standard predictive formula validated by modern metabolic studies is the <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor equation</strong> (developed in 1990):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Men:</strong> BMR = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) − (5 × age in years) + 5</li>
    <li><strong>Women:</strong> BMR = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) − (5 × age in years) − 161</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you use imperial measurements (pounds and inches), convert your units first: divide your weight in pounds by 2.205 to get kilograms, and multiply your height in inches by 2.54 to get centimeters. You can also skip manual math by using our automated <a href="/tdee-calculator/">TDEE & BMR Calculator</a>.</p>

