      <tr><td>Man, 40, 90 kg (198 lbs), 178 cm (5'10")</td><td>~1,920 kcal</td><td>Increased tissue mass requires higher resting cellular respiration</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Woman, 60, 75 kg (165 lbs), 160 cm (5'3")</td><td>~1,350 kcal</td><td>Reduction in lean muscle tissue lowers baseline expenditure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Man, 60, 85 kg (187 lbs), 175 cm (5'9")</td><td>~1,720 kcal</td><td>Preserved through regular resistance training and adequate protein</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Can You Eat Below Your BMR to Lose Weight Safely?</h2>
  <p>Eating significantly below your BMR for prolonged periods is one of the most common causes of diet failure. When calorie intake falls below basal organ requirements:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Skeletal Muscle Breakdown:</strong> When glucose and amino acid availability drops, the liver converts stored muscular tissue into glycogen through gluconeogenesis.</li>
    <li><strong>Downregulated Thyroid Output:</strong> Active triiodothyronine (T3) levels decline, slowing metabolic rate and reducing core body temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Suppressed NEAT:</strong> Without realizing it, your body reduces spontaneous fidgeting, standing, and walking to conserve fuel.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Instead of crash dieting below BMR, calculate your full <strong>TDEE</strong> and create a gentle 300 to 500 calorie deficit. Pair your diet with high protein using our <a href="/protein-calculator/">Protein Calculator</a> to protect lean tissue.</p>

  <h2>Common Misconceptions About Basal Metabolic Rate</h2>
  <p><strong>Myth:"Naturally skinny people always have higher BMRs."</strong> In reality, individuals with higher total body weight (including both muscle and fat mass) have higher absolute BMRs because maintaining larger biological systems requires more total energy.</p>
  <p><strong>Myth:"Fat burners and spicy foods permanently elevate BMR."</strong> Thermogenic supplements produce negligible, temporary spikes in heart rate that have no meaningful long-term effect on BMR.</p>
  <p><strong>Myth:"Your BMR is permanently damaged by dieting."</strong> While metabolic adaptation temporarily depresses energy expenditure during a deficit, resting metabolism recovers once calorie intake returns to maintenance.</p>

  <h2>Calculate Your BMR & Daily Maintenance Calories</h2>
  <p>Now that you understand the Mifflin-St Jeor formula and how basal metabolism operates, calculate your personal number with our free <a href="/tdee-calculator/">TDEE & BMR Calculator</a> to set your optimal daily calorie deficit.</p>

